<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<mei meiversion="5.0" xmlns="http://www.music-encoding.org/ns/mei">
  <meiHead>
    <fileDesc>
      <titleStmt>
        <title>Diffusa est</title>
      </titleStmt>
      <pubStmt/>
      <notesStmt>
        <annot>Mont-Renaud manuscript, gradual and antiphoner, Corbie or Noyon, 10th century; mass of St Agnes, f. 9v. Read from the Paleographie musicale XVI facsimile. Neume division, shapes, directions and facsimile zones: a close reading of the Paleographie musicale XVI facsimile of f. 9v (October 2026), in the French hand without a staff; syllables assigned by aligning the signs with St Gall 339.</annot>
      </notesStmt>
      <sourceDesc>
        <source>
          <bibl>
            <title>Diffusa est</title>
            <idno type="siglum">Mont-Renaud</idno>
            <idno type="shelfmark">Mont-Renaud manuscript (private collection)</idno>
            <ptr target="https://archive.org/details/palographiemusic16macq/page/n105"/>
            <idno type="cantus-id">g01397</idno>
            <note type="genre">Gradual</note>
            <note type="mode">5</note>
            <note label="manuscript" type="description">Gradual and antiphoner, Corbie or Noyon, 10th century; private collection (château du Mont-Renaud, near Noyon). French neumes without a staff, added by a notator later than the text scribe. The gradual of St Agnes, f. 9v. The original is not online: the folio is the facsimile in Paléographie musicale XVI (1955).</note>
            <note label="manuscript" type="title">Antiphonaire du Mont-Renaud</note>
            <note label="manuscript" type="transcription">Read sign by sign from the Internet Archive scan of Paléographie musicale XVI (item palographiemusic16macq, image 0106, cut to f. 9v and turned upright) by two independent readers, October 2026; the neumes assigned to syllables by aligning them with St Gall 339; see research/diffusa-est/staff-readings/README.md.</note>
          </bibl>
        </source>
      </sourceDesc>
    </fileDesc>
  </meiHead>
